# The negative control: the shipped image with the forge binaries removed.
#
# Every assertion in `tests/image/` is a claim that the tier can see a broken
# artifact. Without something known-broken to point it at, that claim is
# unfalsified — the suite would pass identically if `run_in` returned success
# unconditionally. This is the artifact that makes it falsifiable, and it
# reproduces ISSUE-263 exactly: an image where `entrypoint.sh` renders a
# `[developer]` block naming `/usr/local/lib/istota_forge/gh`, and that path
# does not exist. In production that surfaced as `os.execve` exiting 6 with
# ENOENT, at the one point in a task where it would publish.
#
# **Test-owned, deliberately.** The alternative was a build arg in
# `docker/istota/Dockerfile` selecting an empty forge stage — a switch in the
# production recipe whose only purpose is producing a broken artifact. This is
# the same control with no production surface: nothing in a real build reads
# this file, and it cannot be enabled by accident.
#
# Usage — it takes the real image as its base rather than rebuilding one:
#
#   docker build -f docker/test/Dockerfile.no-forge \
#       --build-arg BASE=<the tag tests/image/conftest.py built> \
#       -t istota-test/no-forge:local docker/test
#
# Expected result when the image tier is pointed at it (ISTOTA_IMAGE_TAG):
#   * Group B's literal-path assertion FAILS — /usr/local/lib/istota_forge/gh
#     is not there, which is the assertion that fails on the pre-fix image.
#   * Group A FAILS on developer.forge_binaries, in the environment carrying a
#     token. Under the compose defaults that check SKIPs instead, which is
#     precisely why Group A names its environment.
ARG BASE
FROM ${BASE}

# `rm -rf` on the directory rather than on the two files: an image that ships a
# third binary later should lose that one too, or the control stops matching
# what the product does.
RUN rm -rf /usr/local/lib/istota_forge

# Everything else is untouched on purpose. The config still renders a
# [developer] block naming the paths, the wrappers are still written per task,
# and `command -v gh` still finds nothing — so the only difference between this
# and a correct image is the one the tier is supposed to catch.
